I realized that I wanted to be an  architect when I was seven years old  that Christmas my parents got me a  present but I will never forget hey guys  my name is Danny and in this video I  want to share with you my top three gift  ideas for architects and I'll also tell  you about the best present that I ever  received so for me the criteria for a  gift is really simple  number one usefulness is it something  that I can actually use everyday or is  it something that's just gonna sit there  and collect dust in my apartment most  architects I think are minimalist and  they're extremely organized so they hate  clutter and they hate having things that  are not essential to their lives  number two beauty isn't nice to look at  it's a nice touch is it gonna make you  happy when you see it the last criteria  is meaning what is the meaning behind  the object you're giving if you give  someone bath salt  it could mean I want you to have a  really nice relaxing evening or it can  mean so the first gift category is  notebooks or more skins  a notebook is an essential tool for an  architect and he or she is gonna go  through many of them throughout their  lifetime a good moleskin is high-quality  it's durable and it's much more  beautiful than a standard notebook it's  meaningful because it's something they  can use to develop their creativity and  they'll probably think of you every time  they use it  the tricky thing about notebooks is that  some people can be kind of particular  about it for sometimes for reasons that  you might not even understand out of all  of these can you  which one for gifts personally I like  black notebooks I like having a shelf  full of black notebook it just adds a  little bit of consistency  to my very inconsistent line in fact  this notebook has much better quality  paper than any of the other ones but I  just like having a shelf full of black  notebooks also it took me a couple of  years to figure out which ones I like  the best I used to have these big giant  notebooks without any lines on them but  I found that I was only using a small  portion of the pages then I started  using these tiny little notebooks  because they fit perfectly in my purse  and then I tried horizontal notebooks I  also experimented between blank  notebooks to grid and notebooks and now  I only used on of notebooks I don't  really buy any other notebooks  I like the dot @e fives I like having  the guidance but I find the cards to be  a little bit too bridgid I will put the  links to these in the bottom another  good gift idea is books architecture  books are generally beautifully designed  but are very expensive  here are some classics that every  architecture student will need these  King books I use them even out of school  when I was studying for my licensing  exams and I still use them today as a  reference but keep in mind technical  books are very high in the usefulness  factor but they might not have that wow  factor so if you want to go a slightly  different route inspirational books can  also be a great gift and last but not  least my personal favorite gift category  is a good pen or pencil and today I will  be talking specifically about the  rotring pens wrote rings are like the  Cadillac  it's the pen that you will use and  treasure your entire life it's German  but it's manufactured in Japan so you  know it's really good quality you can  tell it's a road shrink because they all  had red bands on them  the first rotring gift that I got was  from my girlfriends when I was leaving  for architecture school ten years ago  and they came to see me off at the  airport and they gave me this pen and  they said something about using this pen  to do great work the gift was a Road  train 600 pen and it has a full metal  body with the distinct rotring hexagonal  shape and this is the road train six  hundred and leva which means that it has  a speckled finish instead of the  straight black metal they actually don't  make this model anymore unfortunately  but they have the newer versions of the  fountain pens that you can buy and I've  tried them and they're just as good they  just the only difference is that they  have a knurled grip here this one  doesn't and I think this pen has  actually helped me become a better  sketcher and better note-taker  I just love writing with this pen it  just the ink flows so smoothly onto the  pages and it's just a very nice  satisfying writing experience they have  the weights that you just don't have on  a regular pen or pencil you know I kind  of feel like I'm holding a very  significant object of hands it's not  just  it's a tool that you use to bring your  ideas to life the weight of it also  helps you press down onto the pages  without putting too much pressure on it  and it also helps with the balance of  the pen so you can actually write for  longer periods of time  all the rotring full metal body pens or  pencils have this really nice weight to  them which I love and that being said I  would actually love to show you how it  actually writes but unfortunately I  dropped it right on the nib not too long  ago so it doesn't really work anymore  and because it's a special model I  haven't been able to find anyone to  replace it so that's the caveat if the  person you're getting this for is quite  clumsy then this might not be the best  skin for them and so they do have the  newer versions of these that come with  the knurled script here  oh also if you're getting them a  fountain pen make sure to check it has  the refillable cartridge if you get them  the cartridge and the bottle of ink with  the pen and that will make a really fine  gift  so the second rotring that I got was  from my mom a couple of years ago and  this one is the roaring 800 mechanical  pencil and like the fountain pen it is  full metal body so it has a very nice  weight to it and the weight keeps the  pen balanced when you're writing for  long periods of time and if you're  buying this for someone who's getting  to go to architecture school trust me  they will be drawing and writing for  long periods of time so the knurled grip  can be quite handy so I really love this  pencil it's beautifully detailed and it  also has a retractable tip so for  someone like me who likes to carry  around their pens and AFER's or in their  pockets this is a really good safety  feature the hexagonal shape also keeps  it from sliding off tilted tables and I  also found that compared to other  mechanical pencils it's much harder to  break the led on these I bought this  roadtrain 600 for myself because I  thought I had lost the other one and  it's a little bit more affordable than  the 800 but it doesn't have the  retractable tip it's also a slightly  smaller in diameter I I like them both I  think I actually prefer the size of the  600 a little bit more probably because I  have a pretty small hand but I think the  majority of people that I've spoken to  you actually prefer it I mean both of  these full metal ones are extremely high  quality and they'll last you a lifetime  they're in the thirty to forty dollar  range so it's a good price rate gift but  if you are looking for something more  affordable or you're maybe thinking of  getting it for yourself they also have  the 300 to 500 series which is which has  a classic body so it's much lighter but  it's also still very good quality  personally I think any of these like  full metal body pens  what makes really great is it kind of  takes all the boxes it's functional it's  durable and I'm pretty sure the person  you're getting this for will use it  almost every day it's just a really nice  object to have sitting on your desk it's  also very meaningful architects draw and  take notes all the time so this makes  that process much more pleasant the  mechanical pencils are probably a little  bit more practical than the fountain  pens but the fountain pens I think make  a much more memorable gift another last  idea is Copic pins so architects use  these all the time but they can also be  quite pricey you can buy the full set or  you can buy them in a set of five so you  can collect your inventory over time and  if you're getting it as a gift and if  you're getting one of the smaller sets I  would suggest getting some of the  neutral colors like the grays or the  beiges or the oranges because those are  the colors that we tend to use the most  you know just the fact that you've  watched until the end of this video  tells me that you really care about this  person and I'm pretty sure they're gonna  be happy with whatever you get them just  don't get them with your book so if you  found this video helpful please like and  subscribe and I'll see you in the next  video

